// Cryptography, bound to Monocypher.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because dependency resolution is designed and not yet built
// (docs/design/packages.md sections 3 to 6); this package is consumed today with `build-lib` and
// `--lib`, the way any sysl library is.

package {
  name    = "monocypher"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block, and that is the point of this package.**
//
// Monocypher includes `<stddef.h>` and `<stdint.h>` and nothing else -- verified against the
// vendored 4.0.3 source, not assumed from its README. It opens no file, reads no clock, calls no
// allocator and asks the operating system for nothing, so there is no capability to name.
//
// linenoise, the other vendored-C package, needs `posix = true` because it puts a terminal into raw
// mode. The contrast is the useful part: a capability clause is a claim about what a library *does*,
// and a library that does nothing but arithmetic on bytes it was handed makes no claim at all. This
// is what lets the package build for a freestanding target.
//
// The one thing it cannot supply is randomness -- see the README. That is not a capability sysl
// could grant it either; it is an input the caller has to bring.
